# Take the wash count off the products made from wash rows (backend plan 0271).
# post.sh runs `node wash-sizes.js` inside the harvester pod. It selects every
# ACTIVE bound row whose printed size is a wash count (`40 lavados`,
# `2x40 lavados`, `44 Lavados.`), and every product of such a row that is UNIT
# and holds that count as its size. Each product then loses its size, and its
# pack count when it is the pack the row printed, through an ordinary item
# update with the harvester's actor: one catalog transaction per product. No
# price changes, nothing is deleted, no size is set.
#
# The products it corrects are listed for the curator in the ConfigMap
# release-task-0007-review of the namespace (plan 0271, section 5), and the
# rows it notes and does not correct beside them. Neither script prints a
# line of either list.
#
# The deploy unit whose release runs this task (k8s plan 0015 D9).
UNIT="velista"
RUN_UNTIL_STAGING="2026-11-26"
RUN_UNTIL_PRODUCTION="2026-12-03"
# The velista release that carries plan 0270 and this task.
PRODUCTION_RELEASE="0.20.0"
# The task writes only catalog. The harvester database is read (choice C2).
DATABASES="catalog"
#
# The most products the task may change. check.sh counts them with `--count`
# and refuses above this number, and post.sh counts again and passes it to the
# command as `--max`, which refuses too. An empty value is not a number, so
# both scripts refuse it.
#
# Measured read only on 2026-11-19, in both clusters: the task would correct
# 76 products on staging (all through DEZA rows, 2 lose a pack count) and 76
# on production. 80 is that count rounded up to the next ten, for each. The
# rehearsal copy (slot 4) bound no product to a wash row, so it set no number.
# Write other numbers only after reading the count that the check phase
# prints, never to get past it.
MAX_PRODUCTS_STAGING="80"
MAX_PRODUCTS_PRODUCTION="80"
